Apart from the heading, this page holds one line of data. There is no derivation on it.

Heading: "Fortsetzung der Übersicht des gegenseitigen Libellenverhaeltnisses" (continuation of the overview of the mutual relation of the levels). The page continues the table on the previous page, headed "Verhältniss der alten Libelle zur neuen". That table compares an old spirit level with a new one on an astronomical circle at Göttingen, starting in late 1817. Its 1818 rows run from Jan 26 to May 7.

The columns can be read off the previous page:
- date;
- position of the instrument: O = Kreis in Osten, W = Kreis in Westen;
- the mutual angular offset of the two levels, always near 7'21";
- two numbers that fit best as the bubble lengths of the two levels in scale parts. They shrink steadily from about 149/86 in December to about 106/61 in May, as bubbles do when the fluid warms and expands;
- a temperature, presumably in degrees Réaumur, rising from +1.25 in December to +10.5 on May 7.

The entry on this page:
- May 8 bis (a second determination, or the next day after May 7);
- offset 7'21".81;
- circle East (O);
- bubble lengths 93.8 and 51.7;
- temperature 12.2.

This entry is consistent with the series (my checks):
- The eight previous offsets have mean 7'21".60, with scatter from 19".18 to 23".28. The new value 21".81 lies inside that range, so the offset of the two levels is stable.
- Both bubbles shrink further as the temperature reaches 12.2°, continuing the trend.
- The ratio of the bubble lengths is 93.8/51.7 = 1.81. Earlier ratios run about 1.65–1.75. These sit near Gauss's noted "Verh.: 9:5" (1.8) and near the ratio of the scale values 5.31/3.07 = 1.73 noted on the previous page.

Nothing further is computed. The rest of the page is blank. The next page turns to a new topic: dip of the horizon with terrestrial refraction.
